<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.nickfigura.farmfriends.expand</string>

  <!-- Runs the published release, never the working tree. See deploy/release.sh.

    Why a third agent instead of a bigger adopt step in the cycle: the 150s cycle
    budget is consumed by two inherently slow calls (collect ~55s, bulk feed
    ~77s), leaving ~8s and about 14 adoptions per run. The server is not the
    limit - the cycle issues only ~27 calls in 150s against a ~5/s ceiling - the
    latency of those two calls is. Adoption is additive and takes no lock, so it
    parallelises safely alongside the cycle.

    Bounded on purpose: each sprint stops after 150s, and expand.py refuses to
    spend below the coin floor that keeps the TARGET herd fed. Once the herd
    reaches the target herd size the sprint exits immediately, so leaving this installed is
    harmless.

    Rate is set from MEASUREMENT plus a hard lesson about concurrency. In
    isolation adoption saturates at the server: 4 workers 4.40/s, 8 workers
    4.89/s, 16 workers 4.88/s, zero failures. But 16 workers at a 5.0/s cap was
    NOT sustainable alongside the cycle: sprints stalled on slow calls, outlived
    their deadline, overlapped with the next launchd start, and the combined
    connection count drove the server to 504 Gateway Timeout. The cycle began
    failing and herd growth collapsed to +25 animals in 13 minutes.

    Two fixes make this safe: expand.py now takes its own flock (one sprint at a
    time) and arms a SIGALRM it cannot block past.

    Settled at 8 workers / 3.0/s, the highest setting measured CLEAN. At 10
    workers the cycle took occasional 504s, intervals stretched to 15-23 minutes
    and peak hunger began creeping off zero. We are already out-producing the
    leader on rate, so compounding does the rest; risking a repeat of the
    starvation that lost first place to buy ~25% more adoption is a bad trade.
    Measured ladder, all with zero ADOPTION
    failures - the cost always landed on the CYCLE instead, as 504 Gateway
    Timeout:

      8 workers  @3.0/s -> ~2.3 adopt/s, cycle clean
      12 workers @4.0/s -> ~3.6 adopt/s, cycle hit repeated 504s
      16 workers @5.0/s -> overlapping sprints, sustained 504s, growth collapsed

    Adoption is worth nothing if the cycle cannot feed the herd, so this sits one
    step below where the cycle starts failing. The cycle is also far cheaper now
    that collection runs on a cadence (rules.COLLECT_EVERY): a non-collecting run
    costs ~23s instead of ~216s. Raise only with evidence from state/expand.log
    AND no new failures in state/launchd.log.
  -->
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/usr/bin/python3</string>
    <string>-u</string>
    <string>__PROJECT__/release/experiments/expand.py</string>
    <string>--target</string>
    <string>250000</string>
    <string>--max-seconds</string>
    <string>270</string>
    <string>--rate</string>
    <string>3.0</string>
    <string>--workers</string>
    <string>8</string>
  </array>

  <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
  <string>__PROJECT__/release</string>

  <key>StartInterval</key>
  <integer>300</integer>

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>__PROJECT__/state/expand.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>__PROJECT__/state/expand.log</string>

  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Background</string>
  <key>LowPriorityIO</key>
  <true/>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
